WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.820
INTRO:
The top two purposes of every for-profit company

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:08.190
is to offer as much value as possible and
bring home as much profit as possible.

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:13.139
Some companies are particularly good at providing
value while others are phenomenal at generating

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:17.600
profits, but very few companies are able to
strike a balance that pleases the peveryone.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.990
One company that has managed to achieve this,
however, is Visa.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:27.110
Generally, it's the retailer or seller paying
credit card and debit card fees, so most customers

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.490
never bat an eye.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.140
To them, using a card is much more convenient
than carrying cash and change.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.020
And as for sellers, most of them view the
1-3% processing fee as an acceptable investment

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.780
because accepting cards brings in so much
more traffic.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.190
I mean just imagine where online sales would
be if it wasn’t for cards.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.280
On the other side of the coin, Visa is pretty
happy as well.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.830
Even though they only make a small fee on
each transaction, Americans alone spent $6.7

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:57.010
trillion through credit and debit cards in
2019, and 60% of that was through Visa.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:02.860
Aside from pulling in massive revenue, Visa
has 0 COGS or cost of goods sold which means

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.500
that their gross profit margin is literally
100%.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.380
Even after you account for operating expenses
and taxes, Visa is able to bring in 51% of

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.120
their revenue as net profit.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.070
So, here’s the story of the most profitable
company in the world.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.630
BANKAMERICARD:
Taking a look back, the origin of Visa dates

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.280
all the way back to 1956 to Bank of America.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.900
At the time, credit cards were already quite
popular amongst middle class Americans, but

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.690
the system was by no means centralized or
streamlined.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.570
Generally, merchants issued their own cards
that could be used to make purchases at their

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.660
stores and be paid off at the end of the month.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.890
But the problem was that each card was specific
to the merchant, so people had to carry a

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.290
card for every retailer they shopped at.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.060
And at the end of the month, they had to pay
off several bills.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.130
Ironically, the situation is basically the
same today as people use different cards for

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.220
groceries, travel, and gas to maximize on
their points.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.930
But theoretically, people can get away with
just 1 or 2 credit cards if they wanted to.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.370
In the 1950s, however, this was not possible.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.149
Many smaller banks had already tried to create
an all-around credit card, but given their

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:12.169
relatively small customer bases, these never
picked up steam.

00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:15.879
When Joseph P. Williams, the product development
leader at Bank of America, suggested creating

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:18.870
a credit card, executives weren’t quite
convinced.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.400
They feared that not enough Americans would
adopt the system and that merchants would

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.180
fight back, but after a bit of convincing,
Joseph was able to get approval for a soft

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.150
launch in Fresno, California.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.239
The executives felt that Fresno was big enough
to give the system a shot, but it was also

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:37.569
small enough that if things went bad, the
company wouldn’t lose too much money.

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:42.930
Something else to note is that Bank of America
had 45% market share in Fresno, so they had

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.859
the cards stacked in their favor.

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:50.409
Anyway, on September 18, 1958, Bank of America
finally launched BankAmericard by shipping

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:53.329
65,000 cards to Fresno residents.

00:02:53.329 --> 00:02:58.069
EARLY BACKLASH:
The launch in Fresno actually went pretty

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:01.599
well, and Bank of America was feeling comfortable
in expanding the program.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.889
But then, rumors started to roll around that
another major bank was going to launch their

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:06.779
own credit card in San Francisco.

00:03:06.779 --> 00:03:11.109
San Francisco was Bank of America’s home
market, so they couldn’t just let some competitor

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:12.739
beat them to market.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:17.159
Bank of America doubled down on BankAmericard
and pumped up card issuance through the roof.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.530
By March of 1959, they had sent out cards
in San Francisco and Sacramento.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.720
By June, they added Los Angeles and by October,
they had issued over 2 million credit cards.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.950
In terms of popularity, BankAmericard was
a massive success as 20,000 merchants were

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.340
accepting the card.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.629
In terms of the media and profitability though,
BankAmericard was having a rough time.

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:40.889
You see, Joseph had vastly underestimated
the number of people who would take advantage

00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:42.050
of the service.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:46.199
Joseph anticipated that only 4% of accounts
would become delinquent, but the actual number

00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:47.989
came in at 22%.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.589
In the meantime, BankAmericards were the perfect
tool for fraudsters leading to the rise of

00:03:52.589 --> 00:03:53.969
credit card fraud.

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:57.809
Now, Bank of America had thought about this,
and their terms of service specified that

00:03:57.809 --> 00:04:00.729
customers would be liable for fraudulent charges.

00:04:00.729 --> 00:04:05.499
But the media and politicians just ate this
up and destroyed the reputation of BankAmericard.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:10.120
By 1960, Bank of America had racked up $20
million in losses, and Joseph would throw

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.790
in the towel by resigning.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.919
Bank of America, however, was determined to
make this work.

00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:18.671
They had seen how quickly merchants and customers
adopted the system, and they knew that they

00:04:18.671 --> 00:04:20.670
had something massive on their hands.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:24.780
Bank of America sent out an apology to their
3 million customers, and they simply stayed

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.030
the course.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:31.230
By May of 1961, the venture would turn profitable,
but Bank of America didn’t make a fuss about

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.230
it.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:36.440
They let the media continue to paint BankAmericard
as a tool for fraudsters as this simply deterred

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.640
competitors from entering the space.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.440
This strategy worked for a couple of years,
but by 1966, it was clear that BankAmericard

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.640
was making bank.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.690
INTERNATIONAL EXPANSION:
Initially, BankAmericard was only meant for

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.600
California because federal laws restricted
Bank of America from directly expanding to

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:55.600
other states.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.130
But, an up and coming competitor called Master
Charge had figured out a work around.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.510
Master Charge had created an alliance between
a bunch of regional banks to circumvent federal

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.510
laws.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:08.900
Seeing this, Bank of America decided to do
the same thing, and given their brand power,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.410
this strategy was extremely powerful.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.370
Starting in 1966, Bank of America began signing
licensing agreements with banks outside of

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.080
the state, but they soon came across another
federal hurdle.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.530
In 1970, the federal government would outlaw
banks from sending unsolicited credit cards

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.600
to Americans.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.010
So, Bank of America could no longer just send
the cards to as many people as they could

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:30.280
and hope that people would bite.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.590
But really, this was a bigger problem for
competitors than Bank of America.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:38.320
Bank of America had already sent out over
100 million credit cards by the time this

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.320
law came around.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.020
So, the law really just stifled competition.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:46.760
In the meantime, Bank of America took this
opportunity to focus on international expansion.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:51.560
Between 1968 and 1977 for example, Bank of
America partnered with an alliance of Canadian

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.010
banks to issue a credit card named Chargex.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.870
Similarly, they would bring out Carte Bleue
in France and they also brought out options

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.970
in Japan, the UK, and Spain.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:04.700
But as the card spread around the world, BankAmericard
started to become a larger and larger part

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.010
of Bank of America.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.970
BankAmericard was no longer Bank of America’s
card, it was simply a popular card that was

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.320
owned by Bank of America.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.400
In other words, BankAmericard was becoming
an independent business that was no longer

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.130
reliant on Bank of America.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.590
And, Bank of America was actually stifling
the reach of BankAmericard.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:27.730
BIRTH OF VISA:
Here’s the thing, not everyone in the world

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:32.990
is big fans of America especially during the
Cold War and the inflation crisis of the 1970s.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.340
Many international customers refused to get
a card that was associated with Bank of America.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:41.240
If the bank was named Chase or Wells Fargo,
they might’ve had an easier time with international

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.810
customers, but their name was literally Bank
of America.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:49.080
Now, Bank of America isn’t actually a central
or national bank like the Bank of Canada or

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.970
the Swiss National Bank, but the name does
create such an image.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:57.110
Aside from this association, BankAmericard’s
dealings became increasingly messy.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:00.780
As Bank of America signed deals with various
banks around the world, they agreed to varying

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.930
transaction fees, varying licensing fees,
and varying terms and conditions.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:08.310
This made the system extremely unorganized
and difficult to manage.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:12.530
So, in 1970, Dee Hock, the chairman of the
BankAmericard committee, proposed the idea

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.470
of spinning off BankAmericard into its own
company.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:19.740
Bank of America would agree and a new company
called NBI or National BankAmericard Inc was

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:23.600
created for domestic relations and Dee Hock
would be made CEO.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.590
However, Bank of America retained the right
to continue issuing BankAmericards outside

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:31.090
of the US and forming new partnerships.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:36.010
It didn’t take long for this to turn into
a massive mess as well though.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:40.550
By 1972, international partners were starting
to get frustrated with Bank of America and

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:42.389
wanted a more streamlined process.

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:44.790
So, Bank of America would seek help from Dee
Hock.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:49.730
Eventually, they would work out a deal and
create a new company called IBANCO or International

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.360
Bankcard Company for international relations.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:58.450
This new system of IBANCO and NBI was actually
pretty effective, but by 1976, the companies

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:01.770
came to the conclusion that there was no reason
to operate as separate entities.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:07.110
So, in 1976, the two companies merged and
created what we now know as Visa.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.600
MODERN DAY VISA:
Now that all of the background issues were

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.880
taken care of, Visa could focus on improving
the service.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.500
This included supporting more currencies,
offering cash withdrawals around the clock,

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.040
launching the debit card, and so on and so
forth.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.630
But by far their biggest challenge was addressing
credit card fraud.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:28.990
Unfortunately, this couldn’t really be addressed
until technology advanced much more, but Visa

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:31.630
introduced fraud protections services as soon
as they could.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:36.259
In 1993, for instance, Visa started utilizing
neural network technology to better identify

00:08:36.259 --> 00:08:37.779
fraudulent payments.

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:41.350
But their biggest step against fraud didn’t
come till 2004 when they introduced advanced

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.069
authorization.

00:08:43.069 --> 00:08:46.430
Advanced authorization leverages machine learning
and risk management tools to assign a risk

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:49.360
score to every Visa transaction in real time.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.450
For example, if you go grocery shopping every
friday, and one week you go on a wednesday,

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.920
that payment would be assigned a higher risk
score.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.019
Similarly, if you live in Austin, but go on
vacation to New York and make a purchase there,

00:09:00.019 --> 00:09:02.379
that transaction would have a much higher
risk score.

00:09:02.379 --> 00:09:06.850
While this system is quite effective and has
allowed for more consumer friendly fraud policies,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.480
critics have argued that the system is intrusive.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.610
In order to identify these anomalies, Visa
needed to know your purchasing habits and

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.410
travel life pretty well.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.970
Despite these concerns though, Visa’s growth
has never slowed down as consumer spending

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.630
just keeps going up year after year.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:24.779
In 1997, Visa reached $1 trillion in annual
payment volume.

00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:28.579
Within just 10 years though, this over quadrupled
to $4.4 trillion.

00:09:28.579 --> 00:09:32.779
Anyway, in 2008, Visa would go public that
same year, and since then, the stock has run

00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:39.870
over 1100% making them the 13th largest company
in the world with a valuation of $438 billion.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:43.480
And when you control the credit card market
and have 51% net margins, that’s really

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:44.540
not that surprising.

